\DOC GENERAL_SUB_P521_TAC

\TYPE {GENERAL_SUB_P521_TAC : (string * (term list)) list -> int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes the modular-subtraction subroutine call at the current
program point, advancing the recorded machine state to the state after the call
and adding an assumption characterising the result written to the destination
local variable.

\DESCRIBE
Takes {localvars : (string * term list) list}, mapping local-variable names to
their address/size data and used to resolve operand names; an {int} selecting
which of the subroutine's entry variants/offset to step over; and a
{string list} of three local-variable names giving the destination and the two
source operands of the subtraction. The call transforms one goal into one goal:
{
   A ?- t
   ========================================  GENERAL_SUB_P521_TAC localvars n [dest; src1; src2]
   A' ?- t'
}
\noindent Here {t} keeps its shape — an {eventually} goal about the same
postcondition — with its final state variable replaced by a later one, giving
{t'}. In {A'}, the assumptions describing register and memory contents at the
old state are all discarded and re-added at the new state, and the accumulated
{MAYCHANGE} assumption is replaced by one at the new state, typically extended
with the destination's memory component. A new assumption is introduced stating
that, under the hypothesis that the two source values are below the modulus,
the destination value equals their modular difference (phrased over the
integers with {rem}). Existing arithmetic-characterisation assumptions are
retained, though reordered.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of outcome, all on goals of this
one symbolic-execution form.

\ENDDOC
